When a canvas 2D filter refers to an SVG filter, Ladybird resolves the
reference at each draw, applies the list’s functions and references in
order, and draws with no filtering for an external document, a missing
element, a non-filter element, a filter in a display:none subtree, or
an OffscreenCanvas. That matches Chrome 158 in each of those cases but
one: Blink’s CanvasRenderingContext2DState::GetFilter() returns the
filter it resolved first until the filter state is invalidated, so a
filter changed between two draws doesn’t apply in Chrome, as it does
in the spec. And Chrome applies the functions of a list whose external
reference isn’t loaded, where the spec has no filtering at all.

Firefox 151 resolves the filter when it’s set, so a filter changed or
inserted after the assignment doesn’t apply there: Gecko resolves it in
CanvasRenderingContext2D::UpdateFilter(). WebKit 26.5 draws unfiltered
for every reference, in every case the test page covers.

A loaded cross-origin feImage image taints the canvas in Ladybird, in
Chrome 158 (whose SVGFEImageElement::TaintsOrigin() decides), and in
Firefox 151; an image that hasn’t loaded yet draws nothing and leaves
the canvas readable in Ladybird and in Chrome. Firefox taints even a
pending same-origin image. And Ladybird caps the primitives of all the
filters a canvas filter list refers to, together, at 256, as it caps a
CSS filter’s one resolved filter, where Chrome 158 and Firefox 151 still
apply two references to a filter with 200 primitives.
